      //   ],
      //   "images": []
      // },  
      {
        "id": "deep-research-agent",
        "tag": "genai",
        "tagLabel": "GenAI",
        "title": "🔬 Deep Research Agent",
        "desc": "A LangGraph-based multi-agent research system that plans, decomposes, searches the web/arXiv/Wikipedia in parallel, validates claims, and self-reflects before synthesizing a cited answer , paired with a Vue/Nitro chat UI that streams its reasoning live.",
        "detail": [
          "A Deep Neural Networks course project built with Rémi Wysocka and Mary Asha Reddy Boreddy: a research agent that behaves like a careful human researcher rather than a single LLM call , it decides whether research is even needed, plans a strategy, decomposes the question into per-tool sub-queries, searches multiple source types in parallel, extracts and scores evidence claim-by-claim, reflects on whether that evidence is sufficient (looping back to search or re-validate if not), and only then writes a final, cited answer. {{img:agent}}",
          "<strong>🏗️ Two independently deployable services:</strong> a Python/FastAPI backend running the actual LangGraph agent, and a Vue 3 + Nitro frontend providing a persistent, multi-session chat UI that streams the agent's reasoning live as it works.",
          "<strong>🧰 Backend stack:</strong> FastAPI + Uvicorn for the ASGI app and streaming responses; LangGraph (<code>StateGraph</code>, <code>InMemorySaver</code> checkpointing, custom stream writer) for orchestration; LangChain for prompt templates and tool binding; Ollama running <code>qwen3:8b</code> locally as the default LLM (OpenAI supported via config); <code>ddgs</code> for DuckDuckGo web search, the official <code>arxiv</code> client for scientific search, and the <code>wikipedia</code> package for encyclopedic search; Langfuse for full per-node tracing; <code>pydantic-settings</code> for `.env`-driven config; and Poetry for dependency management (Python 3.11–3.12).",
          "<strong>🧰 Frontend stack:</strong> Vue 3.5 + Vite 7 with TypeScript; Nitro as a backend-for-frontend server layer (no full Nuxt app); the Vercel AI SDK (<code>ai</code> + <code>@ai-sdk/vue</code>) for the streaming chat protocol (<code>useChat</code>, <code>reasoning-*</code>/<code>text-*</code> UIMessage stream parts); Drizzle ORM over SQLite/libSQL for persistence; GitHub OAuth for auth; <code>@comark/vue</code> + Shiki for markdown/code rendering; Tailwind CSS v4 for styling; and GitHub Actions CI (lint → build → typecheck).",
          "<strong>🧠 The graph, node by node:</strong> a <em>pre-planner node</em> forces a tool call deciding whether research is required at all, plus a reasoning depth and which tools are relevant , trivial questions skip straight to synthesis, saving latency and search cost. A <em>planner node</em> writes a free-text multi-step research plan. A <em>decomposer node</em> turns that plan into concrete per-tool sub-queries (max 4 each), dynamically filtered to only the tools the pre-planner selected, and switches prompts on loop-back iterations to avoid repeating searches. Three <em>parallel tool nodes</em> then run web, arXiv, and Wikipedia search, each persisting raw results to disk for auditability and skipping cleanly if they have no sub-queries.",
          "<strong>✅ Validation, reflection & self-correction:</strong> a <em>validation node</em> extracts a list of claims from each source , each with a confidence score (1–10), a source ID, and a justification , discarding anything scoring below a relevance threshold of 8. A <em>reflection node</em> is the self-correction gate: it can route back to the decomposer (need different/more search results) or back to validation (re-extract claims from sources already retrieved, with new instructions), capped at a max-loop count so it always terminates. Only once reflection is satisfied does a <em>synthesis node</em> write the final, citation-heavy markdown answer, using conversation history and every validated source.",
          "<strong>📡 Streaming protocol:</strong> every node emits a custom <code>{\"status\": ...}</code> event via LangGraph's stream writer, drawing from a rotating set of human-friendly status phrases so the UI never looks static during multi-second LLM calls. The FastAPI <code>/get_stream</code> endpoint re-emits two event shapes over the wire , <code>{\"type\": \"thinking\", ...}</code> for per-node status updates and <code>{\"type\": \"final_answer\", ...}</code> for the token-by-token synthesis output , which is exactly what lets the frontend render a collapsible reasoning trace separately from the streaming final answer. {{img:reasoning}}",
          "<strong>🔌 Frontend↔backend integration:</strong> the core connecting file is a Nitro route that validates chat ownership, kicks off async chat-title generation via a local Ollama call, POSTs the question to the backend's <code>/get_stream</code> with a 20-minute abort timeout, and runs a hand-written brace-depth JSON extractor over the raw byte stream (needed because the backend doesn't guarantee clean line-delimited JSON). Each extracted event is mapped into the Vercel AI SDK's UIMessage stream-part protocol , <code>thinking</code> becomes <code>reasoning-start/delta/end</code> parts, <code>final_answer</code> becomes <code>text-start/delta/end</code> parts , before the finished message is persisted to the database. {{img:final}}",
          "<strong>🎛️ Model flexibility & persistence:</strong> the base model is <code>ChatOllama(model=\"qwen3:8b\", temperature=0, reasoning=False)</code>, with reasoning mode explicitly disabled for speed and deterministic forced-tool-call outputs; every node resolves its LLM through a small helper that checks the request's chosen model first, so users can pick any locally-pulled Ollama model per chat session from the UI. The graph is compiled with LangGraph's <code>InMemorySaver</code> keyed by session ID for multi-turn conversations, and every node additionally writes plain JSON snapshots (plan, sub-queries, raw sources, validated claims, reflection) to disk for debugging, plus a saved markdown copy of every final answer.",
          "<strong>📊 Evaluation & benchmarking:</strong> to prove the multi-node design actually helps rather than just adding latency, the project includes an LLM-as-judge evaluation harness scoring completed runs on up to 5 dimensions (context relevance, answer relevance, faithfulness/groundedness, decomposition quality, reflection quality) via forced tool calls, plus four deliberately-limited ablation baseline architectures , a single LLM call with no retrieval, a single search + LLM with no decomposition or reflection, an iterative search + LLM with a reflection loop but no decomposition, and a decomposer + search + synthesis pipeline with no reflection or validation. A <code>/benchmark_agents</code> endpoint runs all four baselines plus the full agent on the same question and evaluates each, forming the empirical backbone of the course report.",
          "<strong>🧬 How the architecture evolved:</strong> across roughly 55 commits and six phases , a first working v1 agent (single-pass search → validate → answer, no pre-planning or reflection) with streaming and Langfuse observability added early; a frontend chat UI scaffold built in parallel on a separate branch; a V2 rewrite that introduced the pre-planner gate, switched the decomposer to per-tool queries, and added the parallel multi-tool search nodes; a phase adding the LLM-judge evaluation harness and upgrading validation/reflection to structured, per-claim scoring with the ability to loop back to either the decomposer or validation; a phase adding the four ablation baselines, the benchmark endpoint, and per-request Ollama model selection; and a final phase of documentation and the academic report/slides.",
          "<strong>⚠️ Both services are run natively against a local Ollama instance."
        ],
        "images": [
          { "id": "agent", "src": "media/images/deep_research_agent_pics/Agent_pic.png", "caption": "Deep Research Agent , LangGraph workflow" },
          { "id": "reasoning", "src": "media/images/deep_research_agent_pics/reasoning_steps.png", "caption": "Streamed reasoning steps in the chat UI" },
          { "id": "final", "src": "media/images/deep_research_agent_pics/final_response.png", "caption": "Final synthesized, cited answer" },
          { "id": "uml", "src": "media/images/deep_research_agent_pics/uml.png", "caption": "System UML diagram" }
        ],
        "link": [
            {
              "label": "View on GitHub →",
              "href": "https://github.com/Vishnu-add/Deep_Research_Agent/"
            }
        ]
      },
