      {
        "id": "sight-sphere",
        "tag": "hardware",
        "tagLabel": "Assistive tech",
        "title": "🦯 Sight-Sphere Communicator",
        "desc": "Team-led assistive device for visually impaired individuals using ESP32-CAM, ultrasonic sensors, and a multimodal LLM on Raspberry Pi for real-time audio navigation instructions.",
        "detail": [
          "My bachelor's thesis (B.E. , CSE, AI &amp; ML, Neil Gogte Institute of Technology, Osmania University, 2023–2024): an AI-powered wearable assistive navigation device for the visually impaired. Sight-Sphere Communicator fuses a wireless camera feed, an ultrasonic distance sensor, and Google's Gemini Pro Vision multimodal model to give users real-time, spoken descriptions of their surroundings and navigation guidance , hands-free, through a simple set of physical buttons. I led a two-person team, owning the end-to-end integration between the sensor hardware and the on-device inference pipeline. {{img:device}}",
          "<strong>💡 Motivation:</strong> existing aids each solve only a narrow slice of the problem , a white cane only detects ground-level obstacles directly ahead, a guide dog is expensive and needs long training, GPS apps give directions but no real-time hazard awareness, and basic obstacle-detection gadgets sense nearby objects with no broader scene understanding. Sight-Sphere closes these gaps by combining AI-based scene understanding with real-time distance sensing and spoken audio feedback, so the user gets both a description of what's around them and actionable navigation guidance.",
          "<strong>🏗️ System architecture:</strong> a wearable ESP32-CAM module (paired with an HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor) hosts a lightweight async web server over Wi-Fi exposing three endpoints , <code>/capture</code> for a single JPEG frame, <code>/stream</code> for an MJPEG feed, and <code>/distance</code> for the live obstacle distance in cm. A Raspberry Pi 4 polls these endpoints, buffers the last ~5 seconds of frames, reads GPIO push-buttons for mode/control input, builds a multimodal prompt for Gemini Pro Vision, and turns the model's text response into speech via gTTS and pygame. {{img:build}}",
          "<strong>🔌 Hardware:</strong> Raspberry Pi 4 Model B as the central processor; an ESP32-CAM module for video capture and the onboard web server; an HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor (Trigger/Echo wired to the ESP32-CAM's GPIO) for distance sensing; six GPIO push-buttons on the Pi , dedicated start and stop buttons, plus four mode buttons (Manual, Continuous, Distance, and a manual-trigger button); a speaker or earbuds for private audio playback; and a battery pack powering the camera/sensor rig independently of the Pi. {{img:wiring}}",
          "<strong>🕹️ Three operating modes, selected entirely by button press (no screen, fully accessible):</strong><ul style=\"margin:8px 0 0 20px; padding:0;\"><li><strong>Continuous Mode</strong> , auto-generates a spoken description every 20 seconds using the rolling 5-frame buffer plus the live distance reading, ideal for a running commentary while walking.</li><li><strong>Distance Mode</strong> , only triggers a description when the path ahead reads more than 200 cm clear (and at least 20s have passed), avoiding unnecessary audio chatter when the user is already close enough to an obstacle to rely on cane/tactile feedback instead.</li><li><strong>Manual Mode</strong> , stays silent until the user presses a dedicated trigger button (rate-limited to once per 10s), using a prompt variant that only mentions navigation directions if a walkable path actually exists, otherwise just describing the scene.</li></ul>",
          "<strong>🧠 Software pipeline:</strong> built in Python with <code>google-generativeai</code> (Gemini Pro Vision for image+text → text inference), <code>opencv-python</code> for frame capture/decoding and BGR→RGB conversion, <code>gTTS</code> for text-to-speech rendered to an in-memory MP3, <code>pydub</code> (+ its <code>speedup</code> effect) for adjustable playback speed, <code>pygame</code> for audio playback, <code>numpy</code>/<code>Pillow</code> to bridge OpenCV frames and Gemini's expected image format, <code>requests</code> to poll the ESP32-CAM's distance endpoint, and <code>gpiozero</code> to read the Pi's GPIO buttons. The ESP32-CAM firmware itself is C++/Arduino, using <code>esp_camera.h</code> for JPEG capture, <code>ESPAsyncWebServer</code> + <code>WiFi.h</code> for the non-blocking HTTP server, and <code>pulseIn()</code>-based pulse-duration math to convert the ultrasonic echo into a distance in cm. {{img:testing}}",
          "<strong>🎯 Prompt design:</strong> three distinct prompt variants tailor Gemini's response style per mode , a pure object/description enumeration prompt, a description-plus-explicit-navigation-directions prompt (used in Continuous and Distance modes), and a description-first prompt that only adds navigation guidance when a viable path is actually detected (used in Manual mode). Each mode enforces its own cooldown to avoid overlapping or excessive API calls, and every image sent to the model is accompanied by the current distance reading as text context.",
          "<strong>💻 Desktop fallback prototype:</strong> Initially I built a simplified desktop/laptop version that reads from a local webcam via OpenCV instead of the wireless camera and sensor hardware , useful for development and demoing the Gemini + TTS pipeline without the full hardware harness. It was later packaged into a standalone Windows executable with PyInstaller (<code>--onefile --noconsole</code>) for hardware-free demos.",
          "<strong>🧪 Testing:</strong> 12 structured test cases were performed across the full system , ESP32-CAM Wi-Fi initialization, ultrasonic sensor accuracy, GPIO button detection, video stream access, distance-data access, Continuous/Distance/Manual mode behavior, system startup and mode-switching, service stop, and audio-output clarity.",
          "<strong>📈 Non-functional targets (from the SRS):</strong> sub-1-second average response time from capture to audio output, ≥12 hours of continuous battery life, recovery within 30 seconds of failure, startup within 30 seconds and safe shutdown within 10, IP65-level water/dust resistance, and encrypted handling of any stored user data. {{img:team}}",
          "<strong>🔭 Future scope:</strong> lower-latency real-time processing, additional sensing modalities (thermal, LiDAR), smarter pathfinding/obstacle-avoidance algorithms, haptic feedback as a complementary channel to audio, and integration with smart-home/IoT systems for broader voice-driven environmental control."
        ],
        "images": [
          { "id": "device", "src": "media/images/SightSphere/IMG-20240601-WA0002.jpg", "caption": "The assembled Sight-Sphere device" },
          { "id": "build", "src": "media/images/SightSphere/IMG-20240601-WA0001.jpg", "caption": "Hardware build , ESP32-CAM, ultrasonic sensors, and Raspberry Pi" },
          { "id": "wiring", "src": "media/images/SightSphere/IMG-20240601-WA0004.jpg ", "caption": "Sensor wiring and integration" },
          { "id": "testing", "src": "media/images/SightSphere/IMG-20240601-WA0003.jpg", "caption": "Field-testing the navigation aid" },
          { "id": "team", "src": "media/images/SightSphere/IMG_9800.jpg", "caption": "" }
        ]
      },
